  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Christina's rootshank (Phaeocollybia christinae) is a small, dark fungus belonging to the Hymenogastraceae family, characterized by its distinctive rooting structure that extends into the soil. This species is relatively obscure and primarily documented in New England forests where it plays a minor ecological role.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rooting Structure",
      "description": "Presence of a prominent root-like base or rhizomorph extending into the substrate, which is characteristic of Phaeocollybia species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark Coloration",
      "description": "Dark brown to blackish fruiting body with a relatively small, inconspicuous cap",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Habitat Association",
      "description": "Found in coniferous or mixed forest litter, typically among mosses and needle beds",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Christina's rootshank inhabits moist forest floors in New England, particularly in coniferous and mixed deciduous-coniferous woodlands where it fruitswithin moss mats and organic debris.",
